The entries of an account
19-the-master-files
Three kinds, which K switches between. The VOC entries of the account under the cursor in Accounts are listed in name order, each with its type (V verb, K keyword, F file, Q pointer to a file in another account, R pointer to a record, PA or PH paragraph, S stored sentence, X text) and what it points at. The dictionary items are those of every file the VOC points at, by the dictionary path of the file pointer, each with its file, id, type (D, I, E, L, PH, X) and column heading. The programs are the records of the account's program files (a directory file named bp, or ending .bp, or with a .out file beside it), each with Built: yes when the compiled program is in the .out file under the same name, - when it is not. The box under the lists shows the entry under the cursor field by field.
A > in the second column means the target lies outside this account: in SDSYS (the shipped pointers lead to @SDSYS/voc.dic and the like) or in another account. For a VOC entry the pointer itself is the account's own record and is edited like any other; what it points at is never edited from this page. A dictionary item marked > lives in another account's dictionary and is only read through this account's pointer; it is edited in the account it lives in. The same holds for the programs of a program file that lies outside the account.
/ keeps what holds a part you type, in the id, and in a dictionary also in the heading. F does the same in every account: it reads them all, so it is run only when you ask, and the box then lists the hits with their accounts. Enter on a hit goes to that entry in its account. Esc takes the find and the filter off. F5 reads again.
E edits the record under the cursor in the editor, in the account it belongs to, and N makes a new one (its id is asked first): VOC entries only in the VOC view, dictionary items only in the dictionary view, as the item form. A pointer record is edited like any other; an item of a dictionary that lies outside the account is refused with the reason, and nothing is written. D deletes the record under the cursor, in the same account and under the same rule, after you type its id in the box (Cancel is the default); a record someone else holds open is not deleted. In the programs view E edits the program and N makes a new one in the editor, in its own account; C compiles it in that account (run.in), and so do the editor's Compile and Catalog; the editor does not run or debug it from here. Programs are not deleted from here yet. Each edit writes one master edit line to the audit trail, each delete one master delete line.
